<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toompea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toompea is a limestone hill in the centre of Tallinn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, rising above the medieval Lower Town. An Estonian stronghold stood there before the Danish conquest of 1219, when Valdemar II built a castle on it. It remained the seat of the rulers of the region under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and Toompea Castle now houses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riigikogu">Riigikogu</a>, Estonia's parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toompea.jpg" alt="Toompea" width="320" height="104" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivar Leidus · CC BY-SA 3.0 ee · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tallinn_Toompea_Upper_Old_Town_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Toompea, from the German Domberg or Cathedral Hill, is a limestone hill of about 7 hectares in the centre of Tallinn, rising 20 to 30 metres above its surroundings. It emerged as an island from the Baltic Ice Lake about 10,000 years ago and later joined the mainland. Its upper layer is Ordovician limestone, and cliffs of up to 25 metres border it on three sides. In Estonian mythology, as told in the national epic Kalevipoeg, it is the burial mound that Linda heaped over the grave of her husband Kalev.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A stronghold was built on the hill in the 10th or 11th century by residents of the county of Revala, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia</a>'s chronicle calls it Lindanise. In the summer of 1219 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a> took the deserted stronghold and began a castle there. In 1227 the Brothers of the Sword took it and built the first stone castle, but it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stensby">Treaty of Stensby</a> in 1238, and the cathedral was completed in 1240 where it now stands. Toompea became the centre of provincial authority, clergy and nobility, while the Lower Town gained its own administration, and King Eric IV granted it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> city rights in 1248.</p><p>After the St. George's Night Uprising Denmark sold northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in 1346, and Toompea passed to the Livonian Order, which made its castle a strong centre and completed the first part of the tower Pikk Hermann by 1371. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> took the region in 1561 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> conquered Tallinn in 1710. A fire in 1684 destroyed most of the buildings of the Great Castle, including the cathedral. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> the east wing became an administrative building in 1767 to 1773. Toompea was joined with the Lower Town in 1878, and the Russian Orthodox Alexander Nevsky Cathedral was built in 1894 to 1900.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Toompea is part of Tallinn's Old Town, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Toompea Castle houses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riigikogu">Riigikogu</a>, the Estonian parliament, in an expressionist building of 1922 beside the governor's palace and the medieval walls. The Lutheran Cathedral has best kept its medieval look, the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> sits in the Stenbock House, and the Estonian Academy of Sciences is in the Ungern-Sternberg palace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toompea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reval, the medieval name of Tallinn, grew on a site long used by the Estonians, who had a fort on the limestone hill of <b>Toompea</b> and a harbour that the Arab geographer al-Idrisi probably refers to in 1154.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1219 Valdemar II of Denmark landed at Lindanise, defeated the Estonians and built a castle on <b>Toompea</b>, the start of Reval.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-estonia-1219-1346">Duchy of Estonia (1219-1346)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1219 Valdemar II of Denmark landed with a crusading army, defeated the Estonians at the Battle of Lindanise and built a castle on <b>Toompea</b>; legend places the falling of the Danish flag, the Dannebrog, from the sky at this battle.</blockquote><a href="/state/ravala">Rävala</a></li></ul></section>
